ce:section.ce:para:
At the beginning of fermentation (day 0), anaerobic digestion of MS with enzymatic pretreatment significantly improved (p < 0.05) the VFA concentration in all biochemical methane potential (BMP) trials (Fig. 1
                        ). The initial VFAs is produced by the decomposition of monosaccharide by bacteria attached to the substrate. The 3/2 mixing ratio of the ETv/EAs group had obvious advantages over the others, which meant that the production of monosaccharide in 3/2 group was higher than others. At 0 d, the total VFA concentration reached 3.97 g/L, which was higher than those of the 4/1 (54.19%), 0/5 (98.60%), 5/0 (112.63%), 1/4 (211.29%), and 2/3 (292.49%) groups. In the VFA composition, the concentration of acetic acid, butyric acid, and lactic acid in the 3/2 group was 1.38 g/L, 1.07 g/L, and 0.98 g/L, respectively. The high concentration of acetic acid and butyric acid provided advantageous conditions for AD. On the 1st day, formic acid and acetic acid, which can be easily used by methanogens, were exhausted, only leaving a small part of butyric acid and lactic acid. The acetic acid concentration in the 4/1 group accounted for 47.75% of the total VFA at 0 d, becoming the main component of VFA. With the decrease in the proportion of ETv, the concentration of acetic acid decreased, which may promote the production of acetic acid after pretreatment by ETv. When the ratio of ETv/EAs decreased to 2/3 and 1/4, the VFA concentration decreased, and reduced the VFA composition, indicating that the high proportion of EAs was not conducive to the pretreatment of MS. At 0 d, the concentration of acetic acid in the 5/0 and 0/5 groups increased 130.30% and 238.64%, respectively, compared with the control, and the butyric acid concentration increased by 294.59% and 254.95%, respectively. Acetic acid and butyric acid are small organic acids that can be easily utilized in AD. Butyric acid can be broken down into two molecules of acetic acid, which can be used directly by methanogens (Table 3
                        ).
